// Pennsylvania Dutch (Pennsylvania German, Deitsch) spelling to phoneme rules
//
// The rules follow the Buffington-Barba spelling system, as used by
// Hiwwe wie Driwwe, pdc.wikipedia.org and most published literature:
//  - vowels are long when doubled, when followed by h, at the end of a
//    word and before a single consonant followed by a vowel, and short
//    before two consonants or ck, ch, tz, ng, nk, sch, x
//  - a single i is always short, a single a is short unless before r
//  - ww is [v], v is [f], z is [ts], y is [j], sch is [ʃ]
//  - r after a vowel is vocalised (see the centring diphthongs in
//    phsource/ph_pennsylvania_german)
//  - g between vowels is [j] after a front vowel and [ɣ] after a back vowel
//
// Conditional rules:
//  1  Midwest (pdc-x-midwest)
//  2  Lehigh (pdc-x-lehigh)

// front vowels (g -> [j], ch -> [ç] after them)
.L01 e i ae ee eh ie ei ä

// back vowels (g -> [ɣ], ch -> [x] after them)
.L02 a o u aa oo uu uh oh ah au

// the vowel before these is short
.L03 ck ch tz ng nk sch x


